Create Group 05 — Feedback & Status for the DevKit.

This group belongs to:

Layer 01 — Foundations & Interface Primitives.

Existing groups:

Group 01 — Interaction Controls
Group 02 — Form Controls
Group 03 — Selection Controls
Group 04 — Navigation Controls

Use the DevKit Core Design Constitution as the immutable global foundation.

Do not redefine global design tokens.

Inherit all existing:
- color tokens
- semantic colors
- typography
- spacing
- sizing
- control heights
- radius
- borders
- elevation
- shadows
- icon system
- motion
- breakpoints
- responsive behavior
- accessibility
- focus behavior
- touch-target rules
- density
- Light theme
- Dark theme
- High Contrast
- RTL
- localization
- reduced motion
- reduced transparency

Create exactly these 10 components:

041 Alert
042 Status Indicator
043 Badge
044 Notification
045 Toast
046 Progress Bar
047 Progress Circle
048 Spinner
049 Skeleton
050 Empty State

These must be production-grade reusable primitives.

Do not create:
- complete notification centers
- complete dashboards
- complete error pages
- complete loading pages
- application-specific status systems
- page-level success screens
- application-specific empty pages

Those are higher-level compositions.

The purpose of this group is to establish a unified feedback language.

Users must always be able to understand, where applicable:

- what happened
- what is happening
- whether something succeeded
- whether something failed
- whether something requires attention
- whether an operation is still running
- whether content is loading
- whether there is currently no content

Do not communicate status through color alone.

Every meaningful status must have at least one additional communication method where necessary:
- icon
- text
- shape
- pattern
- position
- semantic accessibility state

Use semantic status categories consistently:

Informational
Success
Warning
Error
Neutral

Do not use success styling for merely informational content.

Do not use warning styling for ordinary neutral status.

Do not use error styling simply to attract attention.

Feedback should be proportional to the importance of the event.

Use the following general hierarchy:

Persistent important information:
Alert

Compact state:
Status Indicator

Small contextual state/count:
Badge

User-specific event/information:
Notification

Temporary event feedback:
Toast

Known measurable completion:
Progress Bar / Progress Circle

Indeterminate activity:
Spinner

Content placeholder:
Skeleton

No available content:
Empty State

Do not interchange these components merely because their visual appearance is similar.

All components must support:

Mobile
Tablet
Desktop
Large Desktop
Ultra Wide

All components must support:

Light
Dark
High Contrast

All relevant components must support:

Mouse
Keyboard
Touch
Stylus
Screen readers

All components must support:

LTR
RTL
Localization
Dynamic text scaling
Reduced motion
Reduced transparency

Feedback must never create unexpected layout shifts where avoidable.

Messages may grow vertically when necessary.

Never hide important information merely to preserve a fixed height.

Long localized messages must remain readable.

Do not force important status messages into single-line layouts.

All icons must have meaningful semantic purposes.

Do not use decorative icons that imply a status that does not exist.

Animation must be restrained.

Do not use:
- bouncing
- flashing
- excessive pulsing
- attention-grabbing animation
- continuous decorative movement

Respect reduced-motion preferences.

Error and warning states must remain accessible to users with color-vision deficiencies.

Persistent feedback must remain discoverable.

Temporary feedback must not disappear before the user has had a reasonable opportunity to perceive it.

Do not use Toast for critical information that the user must reliably act upon.

Do not use Spinner when the amount of progress is known.

Do not use Progress when the amount of progress is unknown.

Do not use Skeleton when there is no expectation that content will appear.

Do not use Empty State for loading.

Do not use Empty State for errors.

Do not use Alert for every minor event.

The feedback system should feel:
calm,
precise,
trustworthy,
immediate,
premium,
and unobtrusive.

All components must be composable with the previous groups.

Test:

short content
long content
localized content
RTL
large text
high zoom
screen reader
keyboard-only use
touch
narrow mobile widths
wide desktop widths
dark mode
light mode
high contrast
reduced motion
reduced transparency

Test all relevant states:

neutral
info
success
warning
error
loading
complete
empty
disabled

No feedback component should visually overpower the primary task unless the situation genuinely requires interruption.
